<description>
Compress two stacked masks into one mask marking positions empty in both.

Input structure:
- The grid is two same-size square masks stacked vertically.
- Each half contains the same background color plus one distinct foreground color.
- Red is absent from the input and is used as the output marker color.

Rule:
- Compare the two halves cell by cell.
- Output red where both cells are the shared background color.
- Output the shared background color everywhere else.

Key insight:
- The output is the intersection of the background masks, not a transformation
  of either colored foreground object alone.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "two same-size square masks stacked vertically, each with one foreground color and the same background color",
  "transform": "for each cell position, write red when both corresponding input cells are background; otherwise write background",
  "grid_shape": "same size as one input half"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid:
    input: 2N x N (two NxN square halves stacked vertically)
    N: 3 [observed]; solver allows any N
    output: N x N
  colors:
    bg: one color shared by both halves (BLACK [observed])
    top_fg: one color unique to top half (MAROON [observed])
    bottom_fg: one color unique to bottom half (BLUE [observed])
    all three must differ from RED; solver discovers them dynamically
    output uses bg + RED only
  halves:
    each half contains exactly 2 colors -- bg and its own fg
    top_fg density: 22-78% of cells (2-7 out of 9)
    bottom_fg density: 22-56% of cells (2-5 out of 9)
    fg regions need not be connected (1-3 components observed)
  transform:
    output[r,c] = RED if top[r,c]==bg AND bottom[r,c]==bg, else bg
    both-bg cells (red in output): 2-5 out of 9 (>=1 required for non-trivial output)

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (task is simple enough)
  steps:
    1. Pick N in 3-5 (use 3 to match eval). Pick bg, top_fg, bottom_fg as 3 distinct non-RED colors.
    2. Create NxN top half -- randomly assign each cell to bg or top_fg with ~40-60% fg density.
    3. Create NxN bottom half -- randomly assign each cell to bg or bottom_fg with ~30-50% fg density.
    4. Ensure at least 1 cell is bg in both halves (so output has >=1 red cell). If not, flip one cell in the sparser half to bg.
    5. Stack top and bottom vertically to form 2NxN input.
    6. Derive output -- NxN grid with RED where both halves are bg, bg elsewhere.
```
</generation_spec>
